[
    {
        "content": "<p>I am interested in contributing and I saw this issue: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/issues/42275\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/issues/42275</a> . I was wondering if this would be a good thing to try in order to do a first contribution? And any pointers to going about it (aside from, of course, thoroughly reading all the contribution guidelines)?</p>",
        "id": 615435843,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1786256356
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just make a comment under the issue like \"I am working on it\" to claim it. You can @me when you are done and I can try my best to give a round of review (or maybe I believe <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"780789\">@Felix Pernegger</span>  is also interested in this).</p>\n<p>If you are not familar with typeclass instances I guess this issue can be a bit difficult for beginners. Another question is which one of the equivalent definitions you should choose. In <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41918\">#41918</a> I used the characterization that every point has a local base of order-convex neighborhoods, so I assume this is a good starting point.</p>",
        "id": 615448866,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1786270261
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To that front, maybe also the following resource would be helpful for the equivalent definitions: <a href=\"https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/4917398/equivalent-definitions-for-go-spaces-generalized-ordered-spaces\">https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/4917398/equivalent-definitions-for-go-spaces-generalized-ordered-spaces</a></p>",
        "id": 615449061,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1786270502
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"669043\">Yongxi Lin (Aaron)</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/First.20contribution.20regarding.20GO-spaces/near/615448866\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>... which one of the equivalent definitions you should choose. In <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41918\">#41918</a> I used the characterization that every point has a local base of order-convex neighborhoods, so I assume this is a good starting point.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yea, that's the first definition that was mentioned in the pi-base reference linked in the issue, so that's the one i was planning on going with. Thanks for the pointers! I will take a stab at it</p>",
        "id": 615462941,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1786284108
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"669043\">@Yongxi Lin (Aaron)</span>  I have reconsidered the definitions and my current plan is to use the definition that a GO-space is a topology that is homeomorphic to a subspace of a LOTS. Based on the link that <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"780789\">@Felix Pernegger</span> provided, I believe that definition will be easier to use when proving the required lemmas, and I think his link also provides the proof that the LOTS-subspace definition implies the order-convex definition (though the proof of the converse may be more involved).</p>\n<p>I am trying to understand the best way to express this as a typeclass, and am down to something like option A or option B, but not sure which is the more appropriate approach (and neither of them really seem to fully work yet):</p>\n<p>option A:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GeneralizedOrderedTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">lots</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">instLotsTopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lots</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">instLotsLinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lots</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">instLotsOrderTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">OrderTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lots</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">subspaceCarrier</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The topology is homeomorphic to a subspace of a linearly ordered topological space -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">topology_homeomorphic_to_lots_subspace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃ₜ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">//</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">subspaceCarrier</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>option B:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GeneralizedOrderedTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">topology_homeomorphic_to_lots_subspace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OrderTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">],</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃ₜ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">//</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Alternatively, I just return to my original plan of using the \"order-convex local base\" definition, but if I do so, I believe the second required lemma might be kind of hard to prove.</p>",
        "id": 616181772,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1786569446
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>order convex local base seems like a better definition to me, since it's not quantifying over a universe</p>",
        "id": 616182454,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1786569787
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>also note the difference between ordered and orderable - sometimes there may not be a canonical ordering or we may not want it as an instance for some reason</p>",
        "id": 616182580,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1786569861
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/First.20contribution.20regarding.20GO-spaces/near/616182454\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>order convex local base seems like a better definition to me, since it's not quantifying over a universe</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I've been looking into this and it seems to me mathlib does not have a direct definition of local bases? Maybe I just missed it? I saw something online about defining local bases in terms of neighborhood filters or something, but I don't see how that works since the requirements for a filter seem a lot heavier than the requirements for a topological local basis. Should I just define a topological local basis as a separate thing, and then use it in the GOSpace typeclass def? Or should I just be like <code>\\forall x : \\alpha, \\exists f : N_x -&gt; t, f n \\subset n \\and order-convex f n</code> or something like that in the GOSpace typeclass def?</p>",
        "id": 616411717,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1786656736
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can define local bases through <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/Defs/Filter.html#nhds\">nhds</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/Filter/Bases/Basic.html#Filter.HasBasis\">Filter.HasBasis</a></p>\n<p>and you can actually find out how I defined it in the theorem <code>nhds_basis_isOpen_ordConnected</code> in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41918\">#41918</a></p>",
        "id": 616411928,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1786656831
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"669043\">Yongxi Lin (Aaron)</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/First.20contribution.20regarding.20GO-spaces/near/616411928\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You can define local bases through <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/Defs/Filter.html#nhds\">nhds</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/Filter/Bases/Basic.html#Filter.HasBasis\">Filter.HasBasis</a></p>\n<p>and you can actually find out how I defined it in the theorem <code>nhds_basis_isOpen_ordConnected</code> in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41918\">#41918</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ok thanks! So here's where I'm at on the typeclass def:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GeneralizedOrderedTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The topology is T1 and there is a linear order &lt; on α such that</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">      every point has a local base of order-convex neighborhoods. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">topology_t1_and_has_order_convex_local_base</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">T1Space</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝓝</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">IsOpen</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">OrdConnected</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Does that look right?</p>",
        "id": 616595056,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1786729105
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think maybe you can drop the T1Space assumption in the definition. You can include this typeclass when you prove the theorems.</p>\n<p>Or if you find out that T1Space is really used in every theorem you want to prove about the GOspace then you should use the extends syntax, similar to how <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/Separation/GDelta.html#PerfectlyNormalSpace\">PerfectlyNormalSpace</a> is defined.</p>",
        "id": 616597056,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1786729598
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1184344\">Nick Collins</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/First.20contribution.20regarding.20GO-spaces/near/616595056\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GeneralizedOrderedTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The topology is T1 and there is a linear order &lt; on α such that</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">      every point has a local base of order-convex neighborhoods. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">topology_t1_and_has_order_convex_local_base</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">T1Space</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝓝</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">IsOpen</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">OrdConnected</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Does that look right?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't you need an existential quantifier for the LinearOrder instead of universal quantifier?</p>",
        "id": 616599953,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1786730384
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>EDIT: I did not see Aaron's comment about T1 when i wrote that, i'll look into that later</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"780789\">Felix Pernegger</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/First.20contribution.20regarding.20GO-spaces/near/616599953\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Don't you need an existential quantifier for the LinearOrder instead of universal quantifier?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I see I probably misunderstood how instance declarations work on the left side of a <code>:</code> in the typeclass fields, i thought that was like requiring a witness of <code>LinearOrder \\alpha</code> in the context.</p>\n<p>Is this the right idea then?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GeneralizedOrderedTopology</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The topology is T1 and there is a linear order &lt; on α such that</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">      every point has a local base of order-convex neighborhoods. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">topology_t1_and_has_order_convex_local_base</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">T1Space</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝓝</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">IsOpen</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">OrdConnected</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 616607747,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1786733000
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes; a second option (which is probably easier to deal with for the majority of applications) is to put the linear order as a parameter, like in OrderTopology</p>",
        "id": 616608487,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1786733281
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>you might want to <code>extends T1Space α</code> instead</p>",
        "id": 616611190,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1786734329
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, I think this fits all the suggestions above, although I admit it's unclear to me when we prefer to parameterize on a typeclass instance vs when we want to extend the typeclass:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">GeneralizedOrderedTopology</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TopologicalSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T1Space</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The topology is T1 and there is a linear order &lt; on α such that</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">      every point has a local base of order-convex neighborhoods. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">topology_t1_and_has_order_convex_local_base</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝓝</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">IsOpen</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">OrdConnected</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 616893262,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1786951985
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Probably at some point, one wants both<br>\n(i.e. compare with metric spaces and metrizable spaces, both are important but in somewhat different manners)</p>\n<p>But for you right now for a first PR, best is to just prove some elementary stuff at the beginning (ideally &lt;200 lines) :)</p>",
        "id": 617084670,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1787017630
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"780789\">Felix Pernegger</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/First.20contribution.20regarding.20GO-spaces/near/617084670\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Probably at some point, one wants both<br>\n(i.e. compare with metric spaces and metrizable spaces, both are important but in somewhat different manners)</p>\n<p>But for you right now for a first PR, best is to just prove some elementary stuff at the beginning (ideally &lt;200 lines) :)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ok, for now, does my last thing above look about right <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"669043\">@Yongxi Lin (Aaron)</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"816344\">@Aaron Liu</span> ?<br>\nIf so, I will try to dress it up with the right documentation and stuff, and then get started on one of the lemmas mentioned in the issue (the other lemma I will leave to another new contributor who expressed interest in working on this)</p>",
        "id": 617270268,
        "sender_full_name": "Nick Collins",
        "timestamp": 1787075548
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe as suggested before you can see if you really need the <code>T1Space</code> very often and you can write <code>topology_t1_and_has_order_convex_local_base (x : α)</code> instead of the forall. Maybe <code>LinearOrder</code> could also be weakened to <code>PartialOrder</code> or <code>Preorder</code> and see how much still stays true.</p>\n<p>But in any case, I would really just advice to you to just playing around with different variants, prove lemmas and see what is convenient etc. That is the best way what \"exact\" definition is the right one for mathlib</p>",
        "id": 617320517,
        "sender_full_name": "Felix Pernegger",
        "timestamp": 1787094551
    }
]